o innovations: (1) a multi-stream task-parallel pipeline programming model that leverages event-chaining and work-stealing mechanisms to fully utilize available hardware resources; (2) a graph-based execution flow with per-stream buffers to ensure memory safety for multiple in-flight jobs running concurrently.\nL19: Extensive evaluations on representative real-world workloads show 1.15--1.44X speedup and reduce scheduling overheads by 18--54% compared to state-of-the-art CUDA graph baselines.\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted by Euro-Par 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:2606.05495】 [cs.DC]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:2606.05495v1】 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.05495†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Zhengxiong Li [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 3 Jun 2026 22:38:01 UTC (626 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled SET: Stream-Event-Triggered Scheduling for Efficient CUDA Graph Pipelines, by Zhengxiong Li and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【16†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.DC\nL48: \nL49: 【17†< prev】   |   【18†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【19†new】 | 【20†recent】 | 【21†2026-06】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: 【22†cs】\nL56: 【23†cs.AR】\nL57: \nL58: ### References & Citations\nL59: \nL60:   * 【24†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL62:   * 【26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL63: \nL64: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL65: \nL66: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL67: \nL68: [Button: ×]\nL69: \nL70: loading...\nL71: \nL72: Data provided by:\nL