y files navigation\nL179: \nL180:   *   * 【75†README】\nL181:   * 【75†MIT license】\nL182: \nL183: More items\nL184: \nL185: # zkDuel\nL186: \nL187: zkDuel is the artifact of the paper zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (HASP '26). It contains matched Triton and CUDA SumCheck prover kernels over prime fields, the drivers that time and check them, and the data behind the paper's results.\nL188: \nL189: Repository: 【47†https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel】\nL190: ## What zkDuel is\nL191: \nL192:   * Matched kernels. Each workload has a Triton implementation and a native CUDA implementation of the same SumCheck round: evaluate the round polynomial at its interpolation points, reduce the partial sums, fold the tables with the challenge. Both backends issue the same kernel launches and host synchronizations per run. The CUDA code builds to a standalone executable; the Python drivers run it as a subprocess, with no Python bindings.\nL193:   * Workloads. 25 constraints derived from zkPHIRE, in seven families, plus a diagnostic tier of 7 polynomials. Both sets are listed below.\nL194: \nL195:   * Fields. Four prime fields in Montgomery form with 32-bit limbs:\nL196: Width  | Prime  | Limbs\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: 32  | 2^32 − 5  | 1\nL199: 64  | Goldilocks, 2^64 − 2^32 + 1  | 2\nL200: 128  | 2^127 − 1  | 4\nL201: 256  | BLS12-381 scalar field (255-bit prime)  | 8\nL202:   * Correctness gating. A configuration counts as a result only when both backends finish and their checksums agree. Both backends are also checked against a CPU big-integer reference computed from the canonical expressions in `src/zkduel/workloads.py`.\nL203:   * Measurements. Median wall-clock time per SumCheck run, GPU-kernel time from Nsight Systems, registers and occupancy, and Triton compile time.\nL204: \nL205: zkPHIRE constraints (`--zk-only` selects all 25; the aliases `zkphire_0` to `zkphire_24` are also accepted):\nL206: Family  | n  | Workloads\nL207: --- | --- | ---\nL208: Verifiable / Spartan  | 3  | `zk_verifiable_asics`, `zk_spartan_1`, `zk_spartan_2`\nL209: Halo2 curve checks  | 3  | `zk_witness_non_id`, `zk_witness_id_point_1`, `zk_witness_id_point_2`\nL210: Incomplete addition  | 2  | `zk_incomplete_add_1`, `zk_incomplete_add_2`\nL211: Complete addition  | 12  | `zk_complete_add_1` to `zk_complete_add_12`\nL212: HyperPlonk vanilla  | 2  | `zk_vanilla_zerocheck_hp`, `zk_vanilla_permcheck_hp`\nL213: HyperPlonk Jellyfish  | 2  | `zk_jellyfish_zerocheck_hp`, `zk_jellyfish_permcheck_hp`\nL214: Opening check  | 1  | `zk_opencheck`\nL215: Diagnostic tier:\nL216: \nL217: Workload  | Polynomial\nL218: --- | ---\nL219: `poly_a`  | `a`\nL220: `poly_ab`  | `a*b`\nL221: `poly_ab_plus_c`  | `a*b + c`\nL222: `poly_abc`  | `a*b*c`\nL223: `poly_aabbc`  | `a*a*b*b*c`\nL224: `poly_abc_plus_de`  | `a*b*c + d*e`\nL225: `poly_abcg_plus_deg`  | `a*b*c*g + d*e*g`\nL226: ## Repository layout\nL227: \nL228:     `src/zkduel/                    Python package: fields and modulus guard (fields.py), canonical\nL229:                                    workload expressions (workloads.py), CPU big-integer SumCheck\nL230:                                    reference (reference.py), 256-bit Triton backend (triton_backend/)\nL231:     benchmarks/field_sweep/        Prime-field drivers used for the paper:\nL232:       triton_sweep.py              Triton SumCheck for all 32 workloads and 4 widths\nL233:       compare_triton_cuda.py       Triton vs CUDA, joined CSV with checksum agreement\nL234:       resilient_compare_triton_cuda.py  Same, one subprocess per case, with a per-case timeout\nL235:       workload_specs.py            Workload metadata and aliases\nL236:       kernels/                     Triton expressions: poly.py (diagnostic tier), zk.py (zkPHIRE)\nL237:     benchmarks/u256/               Earlier 256-bit-only drivers (diagnostic-tier polynomials)\nL238:     benchmarks/wraparound_sweep/   Limb-width ablation with arithmetic mod 2^k (not a prime field)\nL239:     cuda/src/                      CUDA sources; field_sumcheck.cu is the paper's CUDA backend\nL240:     cuda/build/                    nvcc build scripts\nL241:     cuda/check/                    CPU checkers for the CUDA executables\nL242:     scripts/                       setup_env.sh, run_paper_experiments.sh, plot_zkduel.py\nL243:     tests/                         pytest suite (field guard, 256-bit arithmetic, CPU reference)\nL244:     docs/                          Benchmarks, implementation, reproduction, reading the results\nL245:     experiments/                   Data behind the paper and the scripts that produced it\nL246:       README.md                    Index: every dataset, the question it answers, its script\nL247:       h100/                        H100 PCIe, the primary platform: sweeps, diagnostics, validation,\nL248:                                    tools/, patches/, env/, logs/, FINDINGS.md, FINDINGS_TABLES.md\nL249:       a100/                        A100: the full sweep CSV and the A100 PCIe follow-ups (followups/)\nL250:     `\nL251: ## Requirements\nL252: \nL253: The paper's software stack:\nL254: Component  | Version  | Used for\nL255: --- | --- | ---\nL256: Python  | 3.12  | drivers, Triton\nL257: PyTorch  | 2.12.0+cu130  | device tensors for the Triton backend\nL258: Triton  | 3.7.0  | Triton backend (its bundled `ptxas`)\nL259: nvcc  | 12.9  | CUDA executable\nL260: OpenSSL headers (`libssl-dev`)  | 3.0.2 on the H100 host  | SHA3 challenges and BIGNUM in the CUDA executable\nL261: NVIDIA driver  | supports CUDA 13  | PyTorch cu130 runtime\n"
